ERR1 = "Place a handler for others at the end of this task body"


def ids():
    return ("ADA95_6.3.4",)


def name(id):
    return {
        "ADA95_6.3.4": "Published Standards/Ada 95/6.3.4 Abnormal Termination",
    }[id]


def tags(id):
    return {
        "ADA95_6.3.4": [
            "Language: Ada",
            "Standard: Ada 95",
            "Concurrency",
        ],
    }.get(id, [])


def detailed_description(id):
    return {
        "ADA95_6.3.4": """
<p><b>Guideline</b></p>
<p>&#8226; Place an exception handler for others at the end of a task
body.</p>
<p>&#8226; Consider having each exception handler at the end of a task
body report the task's demise.</p>
<p>&#8226; Do not rely on the task status to determine whether a
rendezvous can be made with the task.</p>

<p><b>Example</b></p>
<p>This is one of many tasks updating the positions of blips on a radar
screen. When started, it is given part of the name by which its parent
knows it. Should it terminate due to an exception, it signals the fact
in one of its parent's data structures:</p>
<pre><code language="Ada">task type Track (My_Index : Track_Index) is
   ...
end Track;
---------------------------------------------------------------------
task body Track is
     Neutral : Boolean := True;
begin  -- Track
   select
      accept ...
      ...
   or
      terminate;
   end select;
   ...
exception
   when others =>
      if not Neutral then
         Station(My_Index).Status := Dead;
      end if;
end Track;
---------------------------------------------------------------------
</code></pre>

<p><b>Rationale</b></p>
<p>A task will terminate if an exception is raised within it for which
it has no handler. In such a case, the exception is not propagated
outside of the task (unless it occurs during a rendezvous). The task
simply dies with no notification to other tasks in the program.
Therefore, providing exception handlers within the task, and
especially a handler for others, ensures that a task can regain
control after an exception occurs. If the task cannot proceed normally
after handling an exception, this affords it the opportunity to shut
itself down cleanly and to notify tasks responsible for error recovery
necessitated by the abnormal termination of the task.</p>
<p>You should not use the task status to determine whether a
rendezvous can be made with the task. If Task A depends on Task B and
Task A checks the status flag before it rendezvouses with Task B,
there is a potential that Task B fails between the status test and the
rendezvous. In this case, Task A must provide an exception handler to
handle the Tasking_Error exception raised by the call to an entry of an
abnormal task (see Guideline 6.3.1).</p>

<p><b>Developer's Note</b></p>
<p>The first bullet is automated: each task body is checked for a
handler covering <code>others</code>, the same way <code>ADA95_5.9.8</code> checks a
controlled type's <code>Finalize</code>/<code>Adjust</code> procedure for one, using
<code>ent.refs("Handle")</code> on the task's own entity. Unlike
<code>ADA95_5.9.8</code>, this guideline does not forbid the handler from
re-raising, so a handler that lets the exception escape again is still
accepted &#8212; the guideline only asks that one be present. As with
<code>ADA95_6.2.2</code>, Understand reports a task's own handlers as a flat set
regardless of which nested block declares them, so an <code>others</code> handler
on an inner named block is accepted as satisfying this check even
though it would not catch an exception raised elsewhere in the task's
own top-level statements &#8212; the guideline's own intent is a handler at
the task body's own level specifically, which this check does not
distinguish from one on a nested block.</p>
<p>The second bullet ("consider having the handler report the task's
demise") is not automated: whether a handler's body actually reports
anything is a semantic judgment about what its statements do, not a
structural pattern &#8212; a deliberate, silent swallow of the exception is
just as valid a handler body as one that logs or signals it.</p>
<p>The third bullet ("do not rely on the task status to determine
whether a rendezvous can be made") is not automated here because it is
already automated by <code>ADA95_6.2.3</code>, which flags every use of
<code>'Terminated</code> or <code>'Callable</code> regardless of context &#8212; the same
race condition this bullet describes, not a separate one.</p>
""",
    }[id]


def test_entity(file):
    return file.kind().check("Ada File")


def test_language(language):
    return language == "Ada"


def _task_handles_others(task_ent):
    return any(
        h.ent().name().lower() == "others" for h in task_ent.refs("Handle")
    )


def check(check, file):
    for decl_ref in file.filerefs("Declare Body", "Task"):
        ent = decl_ref.ent()

        if not _task_handles_others(ent):
            check.violation(ent, file, decl_ref.line(),
                            decl_ref.column(), ERR1)
